# cspell:word exfiltration
# cspell:word vercel
# Solana mainnet RPC + WS endpoints. Required — the app fails fast at
# startup if these are unset.
#
# Security note — these are NEXT_PUBLIC_* variables, so values ship in the
# browser bundle and are visible to anyone who opens devtools. If your URL
# contains an API key, treat the key as a public identifier, not a secret:
#
#   1. Use separate keys per environment so a leak in one is contained.
#
#      Local dev:  restrict by source IP (your home/office IP).
#                  This is real protection — IPs can't be forged from a browser.
#      Production: restrict by allowed origin/domain (your prod + preview hosts).
#                  This blocks "another site embedding your key in user
#                  browsers"; it does not stop server-side abuse from a leaked
#                  key. Treat the quota ceiling as the blast radius and rotate
#                  on suspicion.
#
#   2. For full exfiltration resistance, proxy RPC requests through your own
#      server so the key never enters the bundle. Worth doing once cost or
#      abuse becomes a real concern.
NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_WS_URL=

# Optional keyed primary in front of the endpoints above, which then become
# its fallback: a call the primary fails (network error or any non-2xx: 403,
# 429, 5xx) is retried on the fallback, and the primary is skipped for 30s
# after. Set both or neither. The browser console names the live provider on
# every page load. Only the app's own reads use the primary; the MetaMask
# connector keeps the endpoints above.
#
# The mainnet build is the existing Vercel project's production deploy of
# `main` (vercel.json), served over HTTPS; these values are set in its
# Production environment. The two deploys differ only in env:
#
#   Variable                                   Demo (Breakpoint)   Free
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_PRIMARY_RPC_URL / _WS_URL      Helius HTTP / WSS   unset
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL / NEXT_PUBLIC_WS_URL   PublicNode          PublicNode
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_GET_MULTIPLE_ACCOUNTS_BATCH_SIZE  10               10
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_PLATFORM_FEE_BPS               0 (fee off)         0
#
# Free is today's production. The demo adds Helius so a crowd behind one
# conference IP is not rate-limited by PublicNode's per-IP limits; going back
# to free is deleting the two primary variables and redeploying. The batch
# size stays at PublicNode's cap of 10 in both: with a fallback, any call
# can land there.
#
# Required before setting a Helius key here: restrict it to the production
# origin (and any preview host) in the Helius dashboard's allowed domains,
# then confirm a request from another origin is refused on both the HTTP and
# the WSS URL. The key ships in the bundle; that restriction is its only
# protection.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PRIMARY_RPC_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_PRIMARY_WS_URL=

# Target cluster: `mainnet` (default) or `localnet`. On localnet the app
# points at a local validator, forces eCLOB-only routing (the DFlow
# aggregator only knows mainnet liquidity), and swaps in the mock demo mints
# from lib/data/currencies.localnet.json. Set this together with the RPC/WS
# URLs — e.g. for a local validator:
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_CLUSTER=localnet
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8899
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_WS_URL=ws://127.0.0.1:8900
# Leave unset (or `mainnet`) for the production endpoints above.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLUSTER=

# Max accounts per `getMultipleAccounts` call. Required.
#
# This is provider-specific. PublicNode's free tier blocks getMultipleAccounts
# requests with more than 10 accounts in `params[0]` and returns 403
# (error body: "Request blocked. Details: blocked parameter: params.0.#").
# PublicNode doesn't document this in their marketing (which advertises
# "no rate limits"); it's a silent product limit.
#
# The balance fetcher requests one ATA per entry in currencies.json. When
# that total exceeds the value below, the fetch is split into ceil(N/size)
# parallel calls; anything larger than the provider's cap will fail every
# balance refresh on PublicNode.
#
# Raise this if you switch to a provider that allows larger batches.
# Set to 0 to disable chunking entirely (send all accounts in one call).
NEXT_PUBLIC_GET_MULTIPLE_ACCOUNTS_BATCH_SIZE=10

# Platform fee — the integrator's cut, charged on BOTH routes so pricing is
# route-neutral (toggling Best route / eCLOB-only must not change what the user
# pays). One wallet collects from both.
#
# Fee is taken from the OUTPUT token on either route, so the destination is the
# fee wallet's ATA for the trade's output mint. How it gets there differs:
#
#   DFlow route — the swap path adds `platformFeeBps` + `feeAccount` to the
#   /order request and DFlow forwards the fee after the trade lands. The API
#   REJECTS /order when `feeAccount` doesn't already exist, and even an
#   *uncollected* declaration eats into slippage tolerance, so the frontend
#   checks on-chain which fee ATAs exist and only declares (and reports) the fee
#   for output mints whose vault is present. Run `pnpm setup-fee-atas` to create
#   the fee wallet's ATA for every mint in lib/data/currencies.json (and again
#   after adding currencies). A mint without a vault trades fee-free rather than
#   failing, so enabling the fee before every ATA exists is safe.
#
#   eCLOB route — the fee is a `platform_fee_bps` argument on our own `swap`
#   instruction, paid out in the same transaction as the fill. NO pre-created
#   ATA is needed: the program creates the fee account itself via an
#   ATA-program `create_idempotent` CPI, with the taker funding the ~0.002 SOL
#   rent once per (wallet, mint) pair. So `setup-fee-atas` is a DFlow-only
#   prerequisite, and on localnet — where the route is forced to eCLOB-only —
#   the fee flows with no setup at all.
#
# The eCLOB route additionally caps the declared rate on-chain: each market
# carries a `max_platform_fee_bps` (seeded at 100 = 1%, admin-retunable via
# `set_max_platform_fee`). Setting BPS above a market's ceiling does NOT fail
# the swap — the frontend clamps to the ceiling and charges that instead, so
# an over-ceiling value earns less than configured on those markets (and
# nothing at all where an admin has set the ceiling to 0). The SDK logs a
# console warning the first time each market clamps, naming both rates; the
# fee row reports the clamped rate actually being charged, which is the one
# place the difference shows in the UI.
#
# BPS — basis points. 1 bps = 0.01%. 5 = 0.05%, 50 = 0.5%, 100 = 1%.
# Empty/unset = no fee on either route. `make demo` runs eCLOB-only, so setting
# 5 here is enough to watch a real 0.05% fee land in the wallet's ATA.
#
# WALLET — base58 pubkey of the wallet that owns the fee ATAs, and the account
# named as the on-chain `platform_fee_authority`. The per-trade destination is
# derived at request time from this owner + the trade's output mint, matching
# the token program (classic vs Token-2022) recorded in currencies.json — on
# localnet, against the mock demo mint the swap actually settles in. The pubkey
# is a destination address, so NEXT_PUBLIC_ is fine — never put the secret key
# here.
#
# WALLET is also required by `pnpm setup-fee-atas` in both its modes, which
# read it from your `.env.local` — not from this example file. Leaving it
# blank turns the fee off in the app but makes that script fail outright,
# since it cannot derive a vault address without an owner. CI supplies it
# from a repository secret to keep the address out of version control and
# out of the job log.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PLATFORM_FEE_BPS=
NEXT_PUBLIC_PLATFORM_FEE_WALLET=

# Server-side API keys. Optional — unset is the default and changes nothing:
# the browser calls DFlow's developer endpoint and Jupiter's lite-api directly,
# keyless, rate-limited per user IP. A crowd behind one venue Wi-Fi shares one
# IP, so for an event set a key: the browser then calls the app's own route
# handlers (app/api/dflow/*, app/api/jupiter/search), which add it on the way
# to DFlow's production Trade API or Jupiter's keyed api.jup.ag. Neither is
# NEXT_PUBLIC_, so the key never reaches the bundle; the build logs each
# provider's mode by hostname.
#
# Locally, the value is never written here. Put the 1Password reference in
# your .env.local and run `make frontend` (or `make frontend-localnet`),
# which runs the dev server under `op run` whenever your .env.local carries an
# uncommented `op://` line. A bare
# `pnpm dev` passes the literal `op://` string through, and the app refuses
# it at startup rather than sending it as a key.
#
# Vercel: set each as a Production variable before the event, alongside the
# two primary RPC variables above, and delete it after. Each one is read at
# build time to pick the browser's route, so redeploy after setting or
# deleting it.
#
# Commented out so a copy of this file stays keyless; uncomment with your own
# reference (a placeholder like this one fails to resolve).
# DFLOW_API_KEY=op://<vault>/<dflow-item>/credential
# JUPITER_API_KEY=op://<vault>/<jupiter-item>/credential
#
# What the keyed Jupiter route carries. Every browser call is one batched
# /tokens/v2/search for USD price and 24 h market data (token names, symbols
# and icons come from the roster, not Jupiter), issued from
# lib/hooks/useUsdQuote.ts, which folds every mint a page asks for in one
# tick into a single call:
#   /swap        the pair, on mount and every 10 s while the tab is visible
#   /currencies  the whole catalogue, on mount and every 10 s while visible
#   pickers      the whole catalogue, on open and every 10 s while open (the
#                swap rows' token picker and the globe's country picker)
#   /vaults      nothing
# A mount skips mints fetched in the last 30 s, so navigating between pages
# fetches nothing it already holds.
#
# What the routes refuse before the key is attached (lib/server/proxyQuery.ts
# and lib/server/apiProxy.ts). Each accepts only the parameters the app itself
# sends, with each value checked, and forwards a query rebuilt from those:
# Jupiter search takes `query`, 1 to 100 comma-joined mint addresses; DFlow
# /quote and /order take their mints, amount and slippage (and on /order the
# user's key and the two fixed flags), and a declared platform fee must be
# this deployment's own, NEXT_PUBLIC_PLATFORM_FEE_BPS paid to the
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PLATFORM_FEE_WALLET account for the output mint. Anything else
# is a 400. A browser request from another site (`Sec-Fetch-Site` not
# same-origin) is a 403, and HEAD is a 405. Responses are always JSON with
# nosniff; a non-JSON upstream body becomes a `{ msg }`, under its own status
# when that is an error and as a 502 when it is a success.
# None of this rate-limits a well-formed caller: per-client limits are a
# separate design, not yet in place.

# External API endpoints. Optional — when set, each overrides both the keyless
# default and the keyed route above. Use for a staging server or an
# alternative provider.
NEXT_PUBLIC_DFLOW_QUOTE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_DFLOW_ORDER_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_JUPITER_SEARCH_URL=
